</tbody></table></div>
<p>The MET figures come from the Compendium of Physical Activities, the standard reference researchers use to compare activities. Sitting still is about 1.3 METs, so both machines roughly double your energy use compared with doing nothing.</p>

<h2>Calories and Fitness: Walking Pad vs Elliptical</h2>
<p>Walking is the more demanding activity because your legs carry your full body weight. A brisk 3 mph walk sits around 3.5 METs. Most people on a desk walking pad walk slower than that, often 1.5 to 2.5 mph, because faster speeds make typing hard. That puts real-world desk walking closer to 2.5 to 3 METs.</p>
<p>Seated pedaling on an under desk elliptical runs from about 2.0 METs at light, typing-friendly resistance to about 2.8 METs when you push harder during calls or reading. The formula is simple: calories per minute = MET × 3.5 × body weight in kg ÷ 200. For a 180 lb (82 kg) person, an hour on a walking pad at a slow desk pace burns roughly 215 to 260 calories, while an hour on a seated elliptical burns roughly 170 to 240.</p>
<p>So the walking pad does more per hour, but the gap is smaller than most people expect. The bigger factor is how many hours you actually use it. If standing tires you out after 45 minutes but you can pedal seated for three hours, the elliptical wins on total daily movement. For a deeper look at the numbers, read <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-burn-calories/">does an under desk elliptical burn calories</a>.</p>
<p>Steps are the other half of the story. A walking pad logs every step on your watch. Seated pedaling usually does not, so if you want to see what your elliptical minutes are worth in step terms, convert them here:</p>
[[tool:steps]]

<h2>Desk Setup and Space</h2>
<h3>Under desk elliptical setup</h3>
<p>A seated elliptical works with the desk and chair you already own. The catch is knee clearance: your knees rise at the top of each stride, and a low desk or a keyboard tray can block them. Measure before you buy with our <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a>. You will also want a mat under the machine and caster cups under a rolling chair so nothing drifts.</p>
<h3>Walking pad setup</h3>
<p>A walking pad needs a desk you can raise to elbow height while standing on the deck, which adds a few inches to your normal standing height. If you do not own a standing desk, budget for one or a desktop converter. You also need floor space roughly the length of a person lying down, plus an outlet nearby and a plan for where the pad lives when you are not using it.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If your desk is fixed height and you have no plans to change it, the decision is already made. A walking pad under a normal desk does not work.</p></div>

<h2>Focus, Typing and Calls</h2>
<p>This is where the under desk elliptical has its clearest advantage. Seated, your hands and upper body stay steady, so typing accuracy and mouse precision barely change at low resistance. Most owners report they forget they are pedaling after a few days.</p>
<p>On a walking pad, your body moves slightly with every step. Many people can answer email and join calls at 1 to 2 mph, but precise work such as spreadsheets, design or coding often slips. A common pattern is to walk during meetings and inbox time, then sit for deep work.</p>
<p>Noise matters too. A magnetic seated elliptical is close to silent, which is why it is popular in shared offices. A walking pad has a motor hum and the thud of footsteps, which can carry through floors to downstairs neighbors. Our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> guide covers the models best suited to calls.</p>

<h2>Comfort, Joints and Who Each One Suits</h2>
<p>An under desk elliptical keeps your feet in contact with the pedals at all times, so there is no impact. That makes it a gentle option for older adults, people with sore knees or hips, and anyone easing back into activity. If you have a joint or heart condition, check with your doctor before starting any new exercise routine.</p>
<p>A walking pad is still low impact compared with running, but you are on your feet, carrying your body weight and balancing. That is good for bone loading and posture, and it builds the walking stamina that seated machines cannot. It is less suited to people with balance concerns or foot pain.</p>

<h2>Long-Term Costs, Upkeep and Durability</h2>
<p>The sticker price is only part of the under desk elliptical vs walking pad comparison. A walking pad has a motor, a belt and electronics, and owners report that belts need occasional lubrication and re-centering to keep running smoothly. If you skip that, you may hear more noise and see faster wear. Check the listing for the maintenance schedule and the motor warranty before you buy.</p>
<p>A manual under desk elliptical has far fewer parts to look after. Most need nothing more than an occasional check that bolts are tight and a wipe-down of the pedals. The common long-term complaint is a squeak that develops over time, which a quality magnetic model is less likely to have. There is no electricity cost and nothing to plug in, so you can use it anywhere in the house.</p>
<p>Also count what you might need to add. A walking pad may push you into buying a standing desk or a converter. A seated elliptical usually needs only a non-slip mat and caster cups for your chair, which are cheap by comparison.</p>

